HUD opacity 0 during title — probably not painted. Fine.

The `.title-screen` overlay has `.title-glow` with blur and a radial scrim. Let's simplify those for all devices? The visual effect is nice; keep on non-software, but in software disable blur. However, the capture runs software, so paint cost drops.

Implement is-software class and CSS overrides, then test click.

Where to set: after this.software computed in constructor. Also before UI created (body exists since module at end of body). Do:
```js
document.documentElement.classList.add(this.software ? 'is-software' : 'is-gpu')
```

CSS overrides:

```css
/* Software rasterisers cannot afford blur/blend layers. */
html.is-software .grain { display: none; }
html.is-software .grade { mix-blend-mode: normal; opacity: 0.55; }
html.is-software .vignette { opacity: 0.75; }
html.is-software .title-glow { filter: none; opacity: 0.45; }
html.is-software .title-content::before { backdrop-filter: none; }
html.is-software .glass { backdrop-filter: none !important; background: linear-gradient(160deg, rgba(9,32,46,.9), rgba(3,14,22,.86)); }
html.is-software * { backdrop-filter: none !important; -webkit-backdrop-filter: none !important; }
```
Careful: `.title-content::before` isn't a backdrop filter; it's a big radial gradient. The radial is cheap-ish, but our animation blurs the whole title-content (filter blur 5px) for 0.7s → expensive in software. Disable the animation in software:
`html.is-software .title-content { animation: none; }` — removes the initial 0.7s blurred text paint. Good, also stability.

Also title glow opacity and `filter: blur(10px)` removal.

Let's implement and test.

